    IF   `the possession was taken from a Welshman without the lawful judgment of his peers by King Henry or by King Richard`
     AND `the King retains it in his hand, or others to whom he is bound to warrant it possess it`
    THEN IF   `a plea was raised, or an inquest made by the King's order, before he took the cross`
           OR `the usual term of crusaders has ended`
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "excepting those things about which a plea has been raised or an inquest made by our order" and "until the usual term of crusaders".
         THEN `Not entitled`
         ELSE Entitled
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: until, excepting because "until" and "excepting" qualify the respite and have their own branch; this branch is reached by a possession not taken without judgment or not retained, which the sentence does not name).
